Presidential elections were held in the [[Dominican Republic]] on 20 May 2012.<ref name="Cambio">{{Cite web|url=http://www.enterao.com/19/08/2011/%C2%BFpor-que-las-elecciones-seran-el-20-de-mayo-en-el-2012/|title=¿Por qué las elecciones serán el 20 de mayo en el 2012?|accessdate=22 April 2012|date=19 August 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121024104333/http://www.enterao.com/19/08/2011/%c2%bfpor-que-las-elecciones-seran-el-20-de-mayo-en-el-2012/|archive-date=24 October 2012|url-status=dead}}</ref> They were the fifth quadrennial elections for the [[President of the Dominican Republic|presidency]] and [[Vice President of the Dominican Republic|vice-presidency]] since 1998, when a change in the electoral law separated the presidential from the congressional and municipal elections.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.senado.gov.do/masterlex/MLX/docs/1C/2/11/18/A12.htm|title=ANTE PROYECTO DE LEY|accessdate=22 April 2012|work=Senado Dominicano}}</ref>

As specified in the new [[Constitution of the Dominican Republic|constitution]] ratified in January 2010, the presidential elections of 2012 coincided with the election of Overseas Deputies in Dominican expatriate communities.<ref name= "voto exterior">{{Cite web|url=http://www.opd.org.do/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=592|title=Dominicanos en el exterior: de la participación a la representatividad|accessdate=22 April 2012}}</ref> Since 1974 parliamentary and presidential elections had taken place on 16 May every other year. However, the constitutional reform of 2009 stipulated in article 209 that the elections would be held on 20 May 2012 to avoid their falling on a work day.<ref name="Cambio"/> Candidates for the presidency competed for the highest number of votes, with the leader needing more than 50% of valid ballots to avoid a [[Two-round system|second round]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.listindiario.com/la-republica/2012/1/15/218264/Arrecia-luchapor-los-votos|title=Arrecia lucha por los votos|accessdate=22 April 2012|date=16 January 2012|archive-date=17 January 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120117195839/http://www.listindiario.com/la-republica/2012/1/15/218264/Arrecia-luchapor-los-votos|url-status=dead}}</ref>

[[Danilo Medina]] of the ruling [[Dominican Liberation Party]] was elected president with a majority of votes in the first ballot.

This was the first election to involve participation by Dominican citizens abroad. In addition to voting for the president and vice-president, Dominican expatriates participated in by-elections for seven new seats (within three [[overseas constituency|overseas constituencies]]) in the Chamber of Deputies.<ref>{{cite web|url = http://www.dominicantoday.com/dr/local/2011/2/11/38573/Dominicans-can-now-vote-abroad|title = Dominicans can now vote abroad|date = 11 February 2011<!--, 7:31 AM-->|publisher = Dominican Today|access-date = 20 June 2012|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20110224051915/http://www.dominicantoday.com/dr/local/2011/2/11/38573/Dominicans-can-now-vote-abroad|archive-date = 24 February 2011|url-status = dead}}</ref>

== Background ==
In 2008 [[Leonel Fernández]] of the [[Dominican Liberation Party]] defeated candidates [[Miguel Vargas (politician)|Miguel Vargas Maldonado]] of the [[Dominican Revolutionary Party]]; [[Amable Aristy Castro]] of the [[Social Christian Reformist Party]]; [[Eduardo Estrella]] of the [[Revolutionary Social Democratic Party]]; [[Trajano Santana]] of the [[Independent Revolutionary Party]]; [[Guillermo Moreno García|Guillermo Moreno]] of the [[Movement for Independence, Unity and Change]]; and Pedro de Jesús Candelier of the Popular Alliance Party in the [[2008 Dominican Republic presidential election|presidential elections]],<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://eleccionesdominicanas.com/2008/05/17/resultados-finales-elecciones-2008-leonel-5383-miguel-4048-amable-459/2766/|title=Resultados finales elecciones 2008|accessdate=22 April 2012|date=17 May 2008}}</ref> while the Dominican Liberation Party maintained a considerable majority in both houses of [[Congress of the Dominican Republic|Congress]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.perspectivaciudadana.com/contenido.php?itemid=12933|title=Reinaldo Pared presidirá Senado y Julio Valentín a los diputados|accessdate=22 April 2012|date=3 August 2006}}</ref> The main topics of the campaign of 2008 were the issue of reelection,<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.perspectivaciudadana.com/contenido.php?itemid=14476|title=Reto PLD ¿Alternabilidad o reelección?|accessdate=22 April 2012|date=14 March 2007}}</ref> the macroeconomic stability maintained by the Fernández government,<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://economiadominicana.wordpress.com/2008/04/17/el-desbordamiento-del-gasto-publico/|title=El desbordamiento del gasto público|accessdate=22 April 2012|date=17 April 2008|archive-date=18 December 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131218135629/http://economiadominicana.wordpress.com/2008/04/17/el-desbordamiento-del-gasto-publico/|url-status=dead}}</ref> and the alleged corruption which dominated the political landscape.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.hoy.com.do/opiniones/2008/2/3/107166/print-no-pics|title=La corrupción política|accessdate=22 April 2012|date=3 February 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100514003956/http://www.hoy.com.do/opiniones/2008/2/3/107166/print-no-pics|archive-date=14 May 2010|url-status=dead}}</ref> The [[Santo Domingo Metro]] and other national matters were also important topics, especially during the final months of the campaign.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://jmreyes.diariolibre.com/?p=17|title=El metro: La jugada política de Leonel Fernández|accessdate=22 April 2012|date=28 February 2008|archive-date=24 December 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111224074921/http://jmreyes.diariolibre.com/?p=17|url-status=dead}}</ref>

During Fernández's third presidential term several public works projects were carried out, a second line of the [[Santo Domingo Metro|metro]] was opened, tourism grew, and free-trade zones were expanded.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://eltelegramadigital.com/2012/02/leonel-fernandez-indica-crecimiento-economico-fue-de-4-5-por-ciento-en-2011/|archive-url=https://archive.today/20120713151723/http://eltelegramadigital.com/2012/02/leonel-fernandez-indica-crecimiento-economico-fue-de-4-5-por-ciento-en-2011/|url-status=dead|archive-date=13 July 2012|title=Leonel Fernández indica crecimiento económico fue de 4.5 por ciento en 2011|accessdate=22 April 2012|date=27 February 2012}}</ref> There was a constitutional reform,<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://pacoredo.net/1058/culminacion_reformas.html|title=La Reforma Constitucional preconizada por Leonel Antonio Fernández Reyna es la culminación de su programa de reformas estructurales que le diseñaran y pautaran los centros del neoliberalismo para sepultar a la República Dominicana|accessdate=22 April 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150422090543/http://pacoredo.net/1058/culminacion_reformas.html|archive-date=22 April 2015|url-status=dead}}</ref> new judges were appointed to the Supreme Court,<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.acento.com.do/index.php/news/10204/56/Los-nuevos-jueces-y-el-espiritu-democratico-de-Leonel-Fernandez.html|title=Los nuevos jueces y el espíritu democrático de Leonel Fernández|accessdate=22 April 2012|date=14 December 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120417153133/http://www.acento.com.do/index.php/news/10204/56/Los-nuevos-jueces-y-el-espiritu-democratico-de-Leonel-Fernandez.html|archive-date=17 April 2012|url-status=dead}}</ref> and the Electoral and Constitutional Tribunals were created.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.elnuevodiario.com.do/app/article.aspx?id=246179|title=Leonel Fernández promulga la Ley crea el Tribunal Constitucional|accessdate=22 April 2012|date=16 June 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150421215246/http://www.elnuevodiario.com.do/app/article.aspx?id=246179|archive-date=21 April 2015|url-status=dead}}</ref> Several treaties were ratified, including seven bilateral accords with the government of Haiti on the Bolivarian Solidarity Fund to finance projects, border security, commerce, migration, health, the environment, and agriculture, as well as an accord with Colombia on air transport.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.republica-dominicana-live.com/republica-dominicana/noticias/ano-2012/marzo-2012/republica-dominicana-firma-siete-acuerdos-con-haiti.html|title=República Dominicana firma siete acuerdos con Haití|accessdate=22 April 2012|date=March 2012}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://eldia.com.do/nacionales/2011/11/29/68847/Leonel-Fernandez-y-Juan-Manuel-Santos-firman-acuerdos-aereo-y-comercio|title=Leonel Fernández y Juan Manuel Santos firman acuerdos áereo y comercio|accessdate=22 April 2012|date=29 November 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150421232701/http://eldia.com.do/nacionales/2011/11/29/68847/Leonel-Fernandez-y-Juan-Manuel-Santos-firman-acuerdos-aereo-y-comercio|archive-date=21 April 2015|url-status=dead}}</ref> Also passed was a law granting fiscal amnesty to all employers behind on their payments into the Social Security system; nonetheless, the attempt to apply it in its entirety failed.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.hoy.com.do/el-pais/2009/6/23/282642/Promulga-ley-otorga-amnistia-a-morosos-de-SS|title=Promulga ley otorga amnistía a morosos de SS|accessdate=22 April 2012|date=23 June 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090626055612/http://www.hoy.com.do/el-pais/2009/6/23/282642/Promulga-ley-otorga-amnistia-a-morosos-de-SS|archive-date=26 June 2009|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.diariolibre.com/noticias/2007/05/22/i136381_index.html|title=Leonel tiene última palabra sobre SFS tras fracaso|accessdate=22 April 2012}}</ref>

In the [[2010 Dominican Republic parliamentary election|congressional]] and municipal elections of 2010, the Dominican Liberation Party won the majority of representatives in both houses of Congress, while the Dominican Revolutionary Party only managed a few seats in the [[Chamber of Deputies of the Dominican Republic|Chamber of Deputies]] and none in the [[Senate of the Dominican Republic|Senate]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.elnacional.com.do/nacional/2010/5/17/48718/PRD-sin-senadores-OEA-60-se-abstiene|title=PRD sin senadores; OEA: 60% se abstiene|accessdate=22 April 2012|date=2010-05-17|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100520022713/http://www.elnacional.com.do/nacional/2010/5/17/48718/PRD-sin-senadores-OEA-60-se-abstiene|archive-date=20 May 2010|url-status=dead}}</ref>

A 2011 poll found that the majority of Dominicans were unhappy with the government.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.eldia.com.do/nacionales/2011/8/16/59880/Leonel-Fernandez-enfrenta-descontento-popular-en-ultimo-ano-de-gobierno|title=Leonel Fernández enfrenta descontento popular en último año de gobierno|accessdate=22 April 2012|date=16 August 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150421232703/http://eldia.com.do/nacionales/2011/8/16/59880/Leonel-Fernandez-enfrenta-descontento-popular-en-ultimo-ano-de-gobierno|archive-date=21 April 2015|url-status=dead}}</ref> Nevertheless, although many Dominicans believed that Fernández could lead them through the economic difficulties, they showed themselves to be dissatisfied with his program.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://eliaspina.diariodom.com/articulos/2012-01-27/13308-gobierno-recibe-respaldo-del-52-de-dominicanos.php|title=Gobierno recibe respaldo del 52% de dominicanos|accessdate=22 April 2012|date=27 January 2012}}</ref>

The internal crisis of the two main contending parties led to divisions within both of them, unleashing mutual defections among their members.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://enrojoysinrodeo.com/site/?p=874|title=Pactos reafirman el bipartidismo|accessdate=22 April 2012}}</ref> The PRD was afflicted with the most complicated internal conflict.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.cibaoaldia.com/se-agudiza-conflictos-a-lo-interno-del-prd/|title=Se agudiza conflictos a lo interno del PRD|accessdate=22 April 2012|date=25 January 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120616133830/http://www.cibaoaldia.com/se-agudiza-conflictos-a-lo-interno-del-prd/|archive-date=16 June 2012|url-status=dead}}</ref> In spite of agreeing to campaign in a civil fashion in a resolution signed by both parties in March 2012,<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://eldia.com.do/nacionales/2012/3/30/78963/Partidos-firman-pacto-por-la-civilidad|title=Partidos firman pacto por la civilidad|accessdate=22 April 2012|date=30 March 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120331160714/http://www.eldia.com.do/nacionales/2012/3/30/78963/Partidos-firman-pacto-por-la-civilidad|archive-date=31 March 2012|url-status=dead}}</ref> this was not observed and the parties pursued an aggressive campaign strategy.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.elcaribe.com.do/2012/04/20/campana-calienta-mes-las-elecciones|title=La campaña se calienta, a un mes de las elecciones|accessdate=22 April 2012|date=20 April 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120424025843/http://www.elcaribe.com.do/2012/04/20/campana-calienta-mes-las-elecciones|archive-date=24 April 2012|url-status=dead}}</ref>

==Electorate==
6.5 million Dominican voters were eligible to vote in the 2012 election.<ref name=laht>{{cite news |title=Dominican Republic Holds Presidential Election |url=http://www.laht.com/article.asp?ArticleId=508151&CategoryId=14092 |work=[[Latin American Herald Tribune]] |date=2012-05-20 |accessdate=23 October 2013 |archive-date=22 October 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171022141343/http://www.laht.com/article.asp?ArticleId=508151&CategoryId=14092 |url-status=dead }}</ref> There were 14,470 polling places open for the election: 13,865 precincts were located within the Dominican Republic, while an additional 605 precincts were open overseas.<ref name=laht/> Approximately 300,000 [[expatriate]]s, making up around 5% of the total electorate, were eligible to vote abroad.<ref name=bbc>{{cite news |title=Dominican Republic election: Issues and candidates |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-latin-america-18087391 |work=[[BBC News]] |date=2012-05-18 |accessdate=23 October 2013}}</ref> The election board had set up official precincts for Dominicans voters living in the [[United States]], [[Belgium]], [[Canada]], [[France]], [[Germany]], [[Italy]], the [[Netherlands]], [[Spain]], [[Panama]], [[Switzerland]] and [[Venezuela]].<ref name=laht/> More than 100,000 of the eligible voters resided in the U.S. state of [[New York (state)|New York]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Balaban|first=Samantha|publisher=Fox News Latino|url=http://latino.foxnews.com/latino/politics/2012/05/10/new-york-may-swing-dominican-republic-presidential-elections/|title=New York Could Decide the Dominican Republic's Presidential Elections|date=10 May 2012}}</ref>

